  <subsection|Extraction: changes required before mathematical
  acceptance><label|extraction-changes-required-before-mathematical-acceptance>

  The initial extraction draft has substantive defects; it is not yet a
  verified conditional extraction theorem. These were sent directly to its
  author and the parent for correction.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Wrong normalization.> The draft describes the unsmeared
    lattice correlation U as including <verbatim|a_N^(4n)>.
    <verbatim|YM.Family.numeric>, <verbatim|YM.Extraction.lifted>, and
    <verbatim|extendCut> are unsmeared moments without this quadrature
    factor. The factor belongs to <verbatim|Order.smears> / the cell volume
    only. Leaving it inside U changes the continuum quantity under
    discussion.

    <item><strong|Generic is not noncoincident.> <verbatim|YM.generic> in
    <verbatim|Lean/YM/Space/Configurations.lean> requires distinct
    projections in every coordinate, whereas <verbatim|conf> only requires
    distinct points. <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Cyclic> in <verbatim|Patch.lean>
    excludes periodic equality in every coordinate of every pair.
    <verbatim|NoHit> excludes only equality modulo the period of the full
    points. The draft equates generic with ordinary noncoincidence and later
    refers to extension through coincidences. Required extension is through
    coordinate ties while remaining off collisions; the source does not
    require smoothness through actual collisions.

    <item><strong|The size assumptions are too weak for the smearing
    conclusion.> Source <verbatim|Bound.cyclic> in <verbatim|Integrate.lean>
    applies on compact subsets of <verbatim|NoHit>, and
    <verbatim|Bound.volume> on compact subsets of <verbatim|conf>. The draft
    limits the bounds to <verbatim|Cyclic> and generic sets. Compact bounds
    only away from coordinate-tie hyperplanes do not dominate a separated
    test that meets those hyperplanes: concentration in shrinking
    neighborhoods is not excluded. Nullity of the hyperplanes alone does not
    repair this. Restore the stronger source bounds, show the exceptional
    sets have measure zero, and supply the actual uniform dominators. In the
    source, <verbatim|intraperiod> uses a constant times the indicator of the
    whole bounded fundamental box, not the original test support alone;
    <verbatim|ring_bound> uses a compact thickening inside <verbatim|conf>
    for the outer limit.

    <item><strong|Grid-only modulus does not directly permit evaluation at
    fixed dense centers.> The source first defines rounded extensions
    <verbatim|extendCut> on the full position domain and proves eventual
    oscillation on compact sets by thickening the compact set and using the
    uniform rounding error (<verbatim|delta>, <verbatim|each_oscillates> in
    <verbatim|Lipschitz.lean>). Only then does <verbatim|localAscoli> apply
    at fixed dense centers. The draft's Ascoli proof skips this step and
    speaks as if the grid-only functions were already equicontinuous
    functions on a common domain. Rounded extensions need not be individually
    continuous; the proof requires eventual oscillation, which is sufficient
    and should be stated accurately.

    <item><strong|Countability must concern basis signatures.> Arbitrary
    tuples from nonzero finite-dimensional complex label spaces are
    uncountable. <verbatim|YM.Extraction.Indices> in <verbatim|Stencil.lean>
    indexes arity, grade tuple and basis-index tuple. The source diagonalizes
    over these countably many coordinates, then uses the finite multilinear
    expansion in <verbatim|components> to recover every label tuple. The
    draft assumes countability of all label signatures and does not explain
    applicability to the actual family. State the basis reduction and prove
    the finite expansion passage.

    <item><strong|Suitability is only a volume-support condition.> Separation
    from collisions comes from membership in the separated test space.
    <verbatim|Patch.suitable> only places the support strictly inside the
    physical cell. Correct the attribution and retain both conditions in the
    domination proof.

    <item><strong|Separate smeared from point reflection positivity.> The
    closed-cone finite-sum argument is correct if each matrix entry has the
    asserted common limit. But for a nonempty word w the diagonal reflection
    configuration <verbatim|(theta w)w> necessarily has coordinate ties:
    reflection leaves spatial coordinates unchanged. Perturbing w and
    reflecting it preserves these ties. Generic point limits therefore cannot
    pass even these diagonal entries. The source's
    <verbatim|Order.realPositive> in <verbatim|Harvest.lean> proves smeared
    reflection positivity using ordered smear limits and exact finite
    reflection forms; <verbatim|assembles> then obtains point positivity from
    smoothness and the smeared result, through
    <verbatim|YM.Table.Pairing.at_point> in <verbatim|Euclid/RPPeak.lean>.
    The draft must supply this route, or explicitly assume and justify a
    stronger point convergence statement covering these nongeneric
    configurations. Generic extraction alone is insufficient.
  </enumerate>

  The source evidence file also needs declaration-namespace correction:
  lattice moment identities are <verbatim|YM.Family.oriented>,
  <verbatim|YM.Family.anchored>, and <verbatim|YM.Family.real_eval>;
  <verbatim|Extraction/LatticeFamily.lean> is their path, not a namespace.
  Its claim that source smoothness passes through coincident configurations
  must be replaced by the actual off-collision, through-coordinate-ties
  statement.

  <subsection|Finite Wilson law: no substantive defect
  found><label|finite-wilson-law-no-substantive-defect-found>

  The finite action and plaquette formula match
  <verbatim|YangMills.plaquette>, <verbatim|wilsonEnergy>, and
  <verbatim|wilsonLaw> in <verbatim|PublicWilson.lean>. The action uses each
  i\<less\>j face once. Nonnegativity follows from unitarity and the
  diagonal-entry bound. Compactness and continuity make the exponential
  bounded and integrable for every real beta; its strict positivity against a
  probability measure gives positive partition function. Normalization is
  then legitimate. Gauge transformation cancels intermediate vertex factors,
  leaves a conjugate plaquette, and preserves the product Haar measure by
  separate fixed left/right translations on each link. These arguments are
  mathematically correct.

  Corresponding internal declarations directly read:
  <verbatim|YM.Wilson.energy_nonneg>, <verbatim|energy_cont>,
  <verbatim|partition_pos>, the probability instance and
  <verbatim|continuous_integrable> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Wilson/Law.lean>;
  <verbatim|prior_gauge>, <verbatim|law_preserved>, and <verbatim|law_gauge>
  in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Wilson/Symmetry.lean>. The draft explicitly
  distinguishes the finite statements from all outstanding RG and continuum
  estimates, and does not misuse undefined integrals as expectations. One
  editorial integration change is required: its P_p,D_N notation differs from
  the statement's L_p,d_N, and section label <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice>
  differs from the parent reference <verbatim|ex-lattice>.

  <subsection|Assembly: valid conditional
  implication><label|assembly-valid-conditional-implication>

  The OPE inequalities, phase-uniform remainder quantifier, and
  finite-channel sums agree with <verbatim|Hierarchy.OperatorProductExpansion>.
  The trace coefficient and finite mode/track comparison class agree with
  <verbatim|Hierarchy.TraceTest>. The absolute-convergence argument for each
  mode is sound: on a sphere, continuity bounds the homogeneous numerator;
  exponential decay controls infinity, and for mass zero the radial near-zero
  power is a+b+1, which is integrable. The value assigned to the integrand at
  the single massless origin is irrelevant to the integral. The draft
  correctly does not claim this integrability argument proves profile
  exclusion.

  The full-mesh identification lemma correctly uses cofinality of an
  increasing integer subsequence and uniqueness of row limits. Eventual
  suitability is available from the earlier setting; retain that dependency.
  The final assembly preserves the family-before-volume order and one common
  hierarchy, but its joint input already supplies every substantive endpoint
  property. Its explicitly conditional wording is therefore essential. No
  additional mathematical defect was found in this implication at the
  recorded content.

  Further source inspection used <verbatim|Patch.lean>,
  <verbatim|Lipschitz.lean>, <verbatim|Integrate.lean>,
  <verbatim|Stencil.lean>, <verbatim|Ties.lean>, and <verbatim|Harvest.lean>
  under <verbatim|Lean/YM/Extraction>, plus the finite Wilson modules above.
  The attempted paths <verbatim|YM/Wilson/Measure.lean> and
  <verbatim|Action.lean> did not exist; <verbatim|rg --files> identified
  <verbatim|Law.lean> and <verbatim|Symmetry.lean>, which were then read. No
  compilation or new Lean proof was run.

