  All four initial statement requests are resolved: strict increase, linear
  grade injections, linearity in label/test, and existence of a nonzero
  smeared operator are now explicit. The two reflection notation requests are
  resolved. Lattice notation and the parent section reference are harmonized;
  the shortened finite section retains the same sound mathematical argument.

  The newly added <verbatim|ex-rp-bumps> lemma is valid. Fixed positive words
  have open neighborhoods in which their internal distinctness and strict
  positive times persist. Nonnegative smooth unit-integral bumps can be
  chosen in these neighborhoods with shrinking support. For each pair of
  words the reflected/unreflected configuration stays off collisions, even
  when spatial projections agree. Continuity therefore makes the paired
  integral converge to the actual Gram entry. Boundedness on the compact
  product supports gives integrability; the product bump has total mass one.
  For the empty word the zero-dimensional space has one point and the chosen
  mass-one measure is the correct zero-dimensional Lebesgue convention, so
  mixed empty/nonempty pairs are also covered. Entrywise convergence of the
  finite positive matrices gives point reflection positivity. The proof
  deliberately does not use generic point convergence at diagonal reflection
  entries.

  Directly read <verbatim|YM.Table.Pairing.nearGram>,
  <verbatim|Bulb.available>, the local <verbatim|kernel> lemma, and
  <verbatim|at_point> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Euclid/RPPeak.lean>; these support
  exactly this route. The manuscript gives a normal mathematical proof
  instead of relying on their names. The full-mesh identification lemma was
  already in the reviewed assembly snapshot; its conclusion remains valid
  conditional on the separately supplied full-mesh row limits and eventual
