  <section|Supplement: scalar cutoff-error
  recurrence><label|supplement-scalar-cutoff-error-recurrence>

  Read <verbatim|/private/tmp/ym-existence-lattice-20261111/manuscript/existence/flat-estimate.tex>
  and its <verbatim|flat-estimate-audit.md>. Reviewed mathematical text
  SHA-256 after the display-environment correction:
  <verbatim|e0f3e176b299bf922bbd87f2e9adb27f75961c67bc4376e51a8e4aedba4dc422>.

  The finite-interval proposition and proof are valid with their stated
  constants. The choice of mu is positive and no larger than c0; since T-t_j
  is nonnegative, reducing the source's tilt exponent retains its upper
  bound. The modified contraction exponent lambda is positive. The chosen R
  gives d/y_j \<less\>= lambda/2, and the reverse triangle inequality bounds
  the change of y. Thus the polynomial factor consumes at most half the
  remaining exponential decay, giving q\<less\>1. The balance Cq+A=C and the
  terminal bound close backward induction. The argument includes M=0, d=0 and
  A=0 without a division by one of those parameters. The final
  exponential/scale-ratio equality is correct.

  Read <verbatim|YM.RG08.Rate.Stair.slope>, <verbatim|poly_solve>, and
  <verbatim|fade_solve> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/RG08/Rate/Ascent.lean>, together
  with <verbatim|Stair.age>, <verbatim|Poly>, <verbatim|Fade>,
  <verbatim|weight_step> and <verbatim|sep_step> in
  <verbatim|Rate/Stair.lean>. The manuscript is a correctly identified
  finite-interval variant, not a literal transcription of the full source
  theorem. In the source the arrays are uniform over a parameter set X, the
  minimal step is log(3), the age is 2+Z+\|t_i-t_k\|, and the terminal
  contribution is incorporated in the recurrence using <verbatim|stepOnly>.
  The manuscript instead supplies an explicit terminal estimate, a general
  positive gap delta and a fixed padded age R+\|t_i-s\|. Its constants are
  uniform in the finite interval when its displayed input constants are
  fixed, but it does not by itself verify the source application's uniformity
  when Z and the other construction parameters vary.

