  <section|Initial review of the ground-algebra
  chapter><label|initial-review-of-the-ground-algebra-chapter>

  Reviewed parent manuscript/existence/phases.tex, SHA-256
  <verbatim|a7f68251ec76a5864e0fa288ce91a7e1ecdaf5dc60c028df6cccb6e0ccd1d0fc>.
  Direct source reads include <verbatim|YM.OS.Drive.against>,
  <verbatim|YM.OS.QRead>, <verbatim|size>, <verbatim|producer> in
  OS/Train.lean; the projection and spatial-invariance source already
  recorded; the full Ground/Compress.lean and Ground/Moments.lean; and the
  four-cluster argument in Ground/Permute.lean.

  No substantive defect was found in the displayed conditional proofs. The
  train hypothesis bounds the squared norms by B_v D^(4n); the triangle
  inequality extends this to all finite sums with growth D^(2n). Symmetry of
  the formal T=I_sharp I and the supremum argument give the bounded insertion
  estimate without presupposing null-quotient compatibility. Null
  compatibility is a conclusion of that estimate. The source hypothesis QRead
  uses the absolute Gram diagonal, which equals precisely this squared norm.
  The source does not require a uniform B_v over all exterior words.

  The placement conventions check: translating theta(w tau_d v) u by d gives
  theta(v) tau_d(theta w) tau_d(u); choosing positive placements of theta p
  and q with amounts r,s produces the time difference x_time-r-s. The
  whole-word compression is independent of width and placement. For positive
  v,w, the concatenated slab word tau_d(theta v) tau_d(w) is the translate of
  (theta v)w, so compression gives X(v)<em|X(w), not a product with an extra
  intervening projection. The adjoint relation M(theta v)=M(v)> follows, also
  for translated words.

  The large-time spatial-invariance argument correctly uses full O(4)
  covariance, including the label action, and positive time components before
  and after the rotation. Its estimate remains valid for varying transverse
  displacement. The separated-cluster induction makes the same point
  explicitly: its varying transverse unitary fixes the projected limit, while
  contractivity controls the varying vector error. Adjacent time-gap
  divergence suffices for the pairwise time gaps used by the source, by
  finite summation. The four-cluster exchange uses the time/spatial swap with
  displacements in the required order; the preceding rotation identity
  applies with b=e_time+e_space fixed by that swap. Testing between projected
  word vectors then proves commutation on the whole ground space. Projecting
  the dense word span proves cyclicity; commutativity makes that cyclic
  vacuum separating. Empty words and the empty cluster list are included.

  Two minor clarity edits were requested: state that x_0,e_0 denote the
  chosen time coordinate/axis despite the initial time-last convention, and
  insert an absolute value around the final complex inner product in the
  train Cauchy-Schwarz estimate. The existing equality identifies that inner
  product as real nonnegative, so this is a notation clarification rather
  than a false inequality.

  The chapter explicitly assumes the repeated-word bound for each slab and
  full orthogonal covariance; it does not prove either from the Wilson
  trajectory. It also stops before norm separability, the representing
  measure and the phase-table properties, as it should. The new source map in
  phase-interface-review.md records those obligations. No build was run for
  this chapter by this reviewer.

  <section|Phase-selection review and literature
  check><label|phase-selection-review-and-literature-check>

  The two minor ground-algebra edits are present in parent phases.tex,
  SHA-256 <verbatim|862384926a1451e5afccedc8a51f23ee9e2030d661347474a7288e28c5061c6f>.
  Reviewed initial phase-selection.tex, SHA-256
  <verbatim|0c420334bc0d0b240f864d7e81e23411bdcdadad6ad33c38e1a7dbacb5ff27f3>.

  The representing-measure proposition is valid. The algebra is nonzero
  because its vacuum has norm one. Characters on a C*-algebra are continuous,
  so evaluations on a norm-dense countable sequence separate them;
  compactness turns the embedding into a homeomorphism onto its image. The
  positive normalized vacuum functional gives a probability measure, and
  cyclicity/separation from the preceding chapter proves full support using a
  nonzero continuous function vanishing outside a proposed zero-measure open
  set.

  Original literature was independently opened with the web tool:
  <hlink|Williams, A (Very) Short Course on
  C*-Algebras|https://math.dartmouth.edu/~dana/bookspapers/cstar.pdf>, dated
  14 February 2024, Theorem 2.13, printed p. 29, gives the required
  commutative Gelfand theorem; <hlink|Sawyer, Lecture Notes in Advanced Real
  Analysis|https://math.mcmaster.ca/~sawyer/Publications/Advanced_Real_Analysis.pdf>,
  Chapter 6, Theorem 32, printed p. 78, gives representation of a positive
  complex-linear functional on C_c(X) for locally compact Hausdorff X. Here
  compact metrizability ensures these topological hypotheses and C_c(X)=C(X).
  The draft's applications are appropriate. Williams Theorem 2.19, printed p.
  34, supplies continuous functional calculus for a normal element; requested
  its explicit citation at the square-root step proving phase reflection
  positivity. Only theorem statements and the immediately relevant context
  were inspected; this is not an independent reproof of those background
  theorems.

  Whole-word character values obey the barycenter identity. Phase reflection
  positivity follows because the finite Gram combination is X<em|X as an
  operator on the ground space, belongs to the unital C>-subalgebra, and has
  its positive square root in that subalgebra. This supplies an actual
  phase-positivity argument that was still an obligation in the earlier
  source map. The remaining phase regularity and covariance requirements are
  correctly left separate.

  The common countable set of rational-position, finite-basis-labelled
  positive words works in every phase under multilinearity and off-collision
  continuity. Actual raw-label finite dimensionality was checked directly in
  Euclid/Register.lean and Labels/Reference.lean: Ref d is the complex
  function space on Frame.Index=Fin(extra+1). The initial draft's
  basis-expansion sentence needs the brief distinction between free formal
  words and their Hilbert vectors: pair the proposed vector difference with
  every word vector, use multilinearity of scalar correlations, and then
  density to prove the vector equality. This clarification was sent to the
  parent. Positional approximation follows from the norm-square Gram formula;
  all reflected configurations remain off collisions even with spatial ties.
  No phase-dependent choice of approximating basis or rational positions is
  required.

  The almost-everywhere ground-space argument matches Phase/Unique.lean and
  is valid. Its f_n are nonnegative continuous functions of the character,
  dominated by f_0 on compact X. Dominated convergence identifies their
  integral limit using the unselected heat projection. The character integral
  of M(v)*M(v) gives the same squared projected norm, so the nonnegative
  variance has integral zero. Its measurability follows from the pointwise
  limit and the continuous character term, without needing a measurable field
  of Hilbert spaces. The one common countable word family permits the
  full-measure intersection; projecting its dense span yields the
  one-dimensional ground space. Probability ensures that this full-measure
  set is nonempty. This is existence of a selectable phase, not a claim that
  every character phase is already pure.

  <section|Initial one-step flat-packet review: revisions
  required><label|initial-one-step-flat-packet-review-revisions-required>

  Read drafter flat-step.tex, SHA-256 <verbatim|6fcce198b27ac035999152ca92790087ae028a42357d68b0ca75cd7a39a982bd>,
  and flat-step-evidence.md. Read actual <verbatim|YM.RG08.Flat.toward> and
  <verbatim|poised> in RG08/Packets/FlatStep.lean, the full relevant matching
  arguments in Packets/Pushed.lean and Torus.lean, and the
  definitions/estimates in Pert/Bulk/{Projected,Atlas,Discrete}.lean and
  Packets/Lines.lean. The following revisions were sent to the drafter and
  parent before integration.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>The finite-label sums are inconsistent. Source RowCost and ColCost
    each fix both finite labels and sum only the cover coordinate. The
    manuscript defines the column sum over the cover and the input label but
    later inserts another factor m for that input label. Its centering lemma
    states a fixed output label with factor m, yet the convolution proof uses
    that bound as if output labels were already summed. To retain the correct
    m\<twosuperior\>q(2/tau)c/L coefficient, use source fixed-label atlas
    conventions, prove the centered fixed-label row bound without m, and then
    sum over outgoing labels once to get m. The root label sum supplies the
    other m.

    <item>The final proposition needs explicit tau\<gtr\>0,
    0\<less\>=sigma\<less\>=omega, 2sigma\<less\>=Ltau and nonnegative
    costs/r. Saying only that the kernels meet the atlas assumptions does not
    state these independent width conditions.

    <item>Matching and compatible periodization are not defined, and the
    one-paragraph regrouping claim cannot establish the asserted matrix
    product. Define tori Z^4/fZ^4 and Z^4/cZ^4 with f=Lc; matching means the
    output-residue sum at every cover root x equals the matrix row indexed by
    [x]_f. State city covariance T(x+Lu,y+u)=T(x,y). Transpose matching is
    the change of variables sum_k T(a+fk,y)=sum_k T(a,y-ck). Matching of a
    convolution follows by first periodizing the final index and then
    partitioning the intermediate coordinate into finite residue classes. Two
    such compositions give M^T A N. This is exactly the source mechanism, and
    is short enough to write fully.

    <item>Distances must use the cover component of a cover-label pair. The
    initial E(x,v)=sep(x,v) is otherwise ill-typed. In coordinate-path
    telescoping, negative edges use the forward difference based at the other
    endpoint; both endpoints stay within the path length of the root. State
    this to connect the atlas's forward-difference estimate to a path in
    arbitrary directions.

    <item>Absolute summability should be made explicit before using
    neutrality, travel or matching. The uncentered total absolute sum at
    fixed coarse root is bounded by (m q L\<twosuperior\>) r (m c
    L^-2)=m\<twosuperior\>qcr. This independently justifies regrouping; the
    centered estimate subsequently improves the quantitative cost by L^-1.
  </enumerate>

  The weighted geometry and the final finite-matrix orientation are sound
  once these conventions are fixed. The scalar centering inequality follows
  from a exp(sigma a/L)\<less\>=a exp(tau a/2)\<less\>=(2/tau)exp(tau a). The
  root kernel is matched by the Y carrier and the outgoing kernel by the H
  carrier; inherited identities then place N_A on the left and M_A on the
  right, agreeing with the mixed precision identity. The latter is explicitly
  assumed in this draft; the source proves it from more primitive primal/dual
  identities. No independent claim that this manuscript has derived those
  primitives is warranted. Final acceptance of this local proof is pending
  the revised text.

