  <section|Operator regularity and norm separability><label|operator-regularity-and-norm-separability>

  Parent phases.tex now has SHA-256 <verbatim|caa09a94e0eb08b4d5d87728a4d1d283de508c9bf72d800bbef2080de9e38d64>.
  Its new rotation clarification matches <verbatim|YM.OS.Ground.rotate> and
  <verbatim|trans> in Ground/Lean.lean: rotation acts on quotient field
  labels, which may be returned to raw coordinates by arbitrary
  representative choices; invariance of correlations makes those choices
  immaterial. An honest raw-label O(4) action is not assumed. The conditional
  ground-algebra proofs need only the stated covariance and its
  translation/concatenation compatibility.

  Read parent slab-regularity.tex, SHA-256
  <verbatim|30403cd8854c4546485ba675a3d5abcb2a07815323fdb01d35637f7573c789a2>,
  against the full <verbatim|Lean/YM/Work/B02/Analysis.lean> and
  <verbatim|Slab.lean>. No substantive mathematical or source-correspondence
  defect was identified. The matrix-element remainder argument applies the
  real mean-value inequality to complex scalar observations and then uses
  density to obtain the operator norm bound. It does not presuppose
  operator-norm continuity of the operator family. The dense test space
  consists of finite word sums, and the derivative bounds scale with their
  actual Hilbert norms, as required; a mere list of individual word estimates
  would not suffice.

  At a fixed slab configuration the finite positive time margins and sup-norm
  pair distances give a common neighborhood with positive buffer. AllCopy
  bounds each fixed derivative-labelled tuple locally, even when the chosen
  raw representative maps are nonlinear. The derivative candidate is
  real-linear in the coordinate increment because it is a finite sum against
  coordinate functionals. Scalar smoothness and the exact slot identity,
  including exterior words, give its matrix elements. The first remainder
  estimate proves norm continuity of every tuple map. Applying this to the
  finitely many derivative tuples proves continuity of the derivative
  candidate and hence the true operator-norm derivative. Induction
  simultaneously over all label tuples then gives every finite derivative
  order. This reproduces the source's <verbatim|columnD_bound>,
  <verbatim|plant_deriv>, <verbatim|cont_place>, <verbatim|deriv_place>, and
  <verbatim|smooth_place> logic without assuming continuity at the stage
  where it must be proved.

  The norm-separability corollary is valid: scalar multilinearity first gives
  bounded-operator multilinearity by testing on two dense word spans. Every
  generator can be placed in positive time, put in a rational-width slab,
  expanded in finite bases, and approximated in operator norm at rational
  positions. Compression is bounded and therefore retains this convergence.
  Rational-complex star polynomials in the resulting countable generator set
  are countable and dense. This supplies the precise norm-separability
  requirement of the character construction from the displayed regularity
  inputs; Hilbert-space separability alone would not have supplied it.

  The chapter still assumes existence of bounded slab insertions, the uniform
  AllCopy estimate, smooth off-collision scalar correlations and exact
  raw-slot derivatives. It proves operator-norm smoothness and algebra norm
  separability from those inputs, not their lattice origin. No Lean or LaTeX
  build was performed by this reviewer.

  <section|Smooth full-mesh convergence><label|smooth-full-mesh-convergence>

  Reviewed parent smooth-limits.tex, initially SHA-256
  <verbatim|d11c61bbbe71c2410791aa48225d42b984f53b492921c87f3c726bed1c1f7161>,
  and re-read the final reference-corrected version
  <verbatim|d7e499a5b4984759780fb64d9cec3949f15f170e246564c5b14853fd31278c9d>.
  No substantive defect was found in this conditional analytic theorem. Read
  the relevant actual FullVolume/Discrete.lean FixedBounds definition and
  FullVolume/{Region,Tuned,Wish,Strings}.lean, with Profiles already
  inspected. The manuscript isolates a local fixed-open-set sufficient
  hypothesis; it does not claim to construct the stronger uniform source
  budgets.

  Monotone coordinate paths give the grid Lipschitz estimate, with negative
  edges evaluated at their lower endpoints. Applying that estimate to first
  differences gives the quadratic discrete Taylor remainder. Rounded
  functions are only asymptotically equicontinuous, and the finite-cover
  Cauchy argument correctly uses that weaker property. The derivative
  convergence argument fixes a small physical h before taking N large,
  compares the next difference with a quotient over h_N, and then makes h
  arbitrarily small; it obtains Cauchy convergence of the entire sequence
  without extracting a new subsequence. Passing the discrete remainder to the
  limit identifies a true Frechet derivative, and simultaneous induction over
  all finite coordinate lists gives smoothness. Moving grid points and fixed
  forward/backward/centered stencils follow with the stated full-interval
  normalization. The dense Cauchy input and every finite-order local
  difference budget remain explicit construction inputs.

