  The exponential multiplier of a compact frequency test varies
  differentiably in every Schwartz seminorm. Fourier differentiation gives
  the stated minus sign relative to D_y=\<minus\>i partial_y. The common
  continuous seminorm bound for the family psi_y justifies the variable-test
  chain rule and its boundary limit; pointwise continuity alone would not
  have justified that step. The weakly ordered separating ray plus fixed
  strictly ordered offset preserves a unit gap, while the compact frequency
  test and all its derivatives decay exponentially against polynomial
  factors. This proves vanishing on each negative half-space. The suffix
  decomposition and constant positive/negative height tests give exactly the
  absolute-coordinate half-space support and zero total time momentum.

  The initial generic phrase real Lorentz group was insufficiently precise
  for the all-frames cone characterization. The final version uses the actual
  proper orthochronous group, and explains that inverse transpose again
  ranges over it. This matches Mink.Proper, whose definition requires
  determinant one, preservation of the Minkowski quadratic form, and
  preservation of the future cone. Conditional covariance then gives the
  future-cone suffix support and zero total four-momentum by the explicit
  boost test. Lorentz covariance itself and the Hilbert-space
  projection-valued spectral measure remain separate inputs; this chapter
  proves neither by naming the support condition. No Lean or LaTeX build or
  blueprint checker was run by this reviewer.

