  <section|Tested Lorentz covariance><label|tested-lorentz-covariance>

  Reviewed parent lorentz-covariance.tex, final SHA-256
  <verbatim|1d7aa3ba37c8bdd50ccad07dcd31fa94ed9be695fb646ab42f650aff4c15a437>.
  Read Work/C07 top-level and its Continue/Integrate components, the relevant
  C09 continuation and moving-evaluation proofs, and A16. No remaining
  substantive local defect was found. The compatible finite-level Lorentz
  label representation remains an explicit input, including boost generator
  K_a=-iL_a.

  The scaled complex parameter domain is open and convex because compact test
  support makes all ordered-height inequalities uniform. At imaginary
  parameter i sigma, the vector field x partial_t\<minus\>sigma(y+sigma t)
  partial_x is divergence free and transforms to sigma times the Euclidean
  rotation generator; its derivative of the compactly supported product gives
  the correctly signed Ward identity. The one-variable identity theorem
  extends this to parameter one. Cutoffs converge in every Schwartz seminorm,
  and the common boundary seminorm bound controls the vanishing
  imaginary-height correction.

  The affine pullback continuity proof includes a compact-ball/tail argument;
  its boost derivative has a uniform second-order Taylor bound in every
  Schwartz seminorm. The label representation law and derivative at zero give
  the finite-dimensional differential equation at every boost parameter. The
  scalar distributional Ward identity therefore integrates to boost
  covariance. Spatial rotations and translations are inherited by
  continuation. The explicit coordinate-boost reduction of a future unit
  timelike vector to rest, followed by the SO(3) stabilizer, supplies the
  actual proper orthochronous group. This discharges the conditional
  covariance input of the Fourier-support chapter, subject to the
  still-explicit compatible label action.

