  <section|Averaged homogeneous OPE prescription><label|averaged-homogeneous-ope-prescription>

  Reviewed parent ope-homogeneous.tex, SHA-256
  <verbatim|621f8e99c7f2dce813333c8fbe022cfabef7f47a457ada36e707f08aab62b7e7>,
  against full OPE/Orbits/{Port,Spin,Average,Real,Cuts,Fresh,Stars,System}.lean
  at the pinned source revision. No substantive mathematical or main-step
  correspondence defect was found. Compactness of O(4) gives the open common
  orbit domain and a compact neighborhood with uniform positive separation.
  Finite-basis expansion and continuous bounded representation matrices
  justify every position derivative under the Haar integral without assuming
  differentiability in the group variable. The left Haar substitution,
  reality average, singleton identity and inclusion compatibility hold with
  the displayed orientations.

  The recursive K_m and P_mq maps follow the source's knife/cutTest/fresh
  construction. Low-degree reconstruction first implies that higher
  projections kill lower degrees. Strong induction then puts each K_m range
  in the intersection of earlier projection kernels: only the equal-index
  summand survives. This establishes the direct sum and exact projection of
  every finite packet, including when the separate zero-extended coefficient
  domains differ. The conjugation-fixed real basis does complexify to the
  full stratum, and rotation covariance is correctly stated for the
  field-valued output rather than for each scalar coefficient. Spectator
  evaluation is the exact physical-inclusion identity and retains phase
  independence of the chosen coefficients.

  This chapter proves the covariant homogeneous prescription under the
  preceding actual field/correlation data. It does not prove a quantitative
  OPE remainder, and its final paragraph explicitly retains that obligation.
  No builds, metadata approvals or source edits were made.

  <section|Fixed-clearance quantitative OPE
  transfer><label|fixed-clearance-quantitative-ope-transfer>

