  b=c=0, not the stronger multiscale rate theorem. Finite-basis expansion
  gives the required varying detector-label and varying-field evaluation
  bounds. The rotated raw-error identity has the correct forward detector
  rotation and output action. Compactness supplies bounded labels; the
  bad-rotation sequence argument supplies uniformity before integration.

  The conjugated comparison uses J_D H_D of the original cluster, not a
  recomputed raw shadow; complete-correlation conjugation identifies its
  discrepancy with the original discrepancy evaluated at conjugated
  spectators. Thus the reality averaging bridge is valid without a false
  reality property of the arbitrary raw prescription. Applying the fixed
  homogeneous projection and killing all lower degrees gives the coefficient
  rate. The full error is exactly the raw discrepancy plus the sum of
  projected-shadow differences evaluated by the same spectator functionals.
  Their fixed-clearance rate zero yields the claimed remainder rate. Bad
  configurations and phases chosen along radii tending to zero give the
  common radius threshold. Empty exteriors and complete unit/disconnected
  terms are retained.

  The displayed raw coefficient, full discrepancy and separated insertion
  rate assumptions remain substantive quantitative inputs; this chapter
  transfers them and does not produce them from Wilson measures. No source
  edits, builds or metadata approvals were made.

