  <section|Finite local sections and their uniform
  prices><label|finite-local-sections-and-their-uniform-prices>

  Reviewed parent local-sections.tex at SHA-256
  <verbatim|a9987194194cc332b06e0b4d58e7420ff33169663e10914ec80fe41f096397ac>.
  Inspected the relevant G01 sampler declarations, full G03--G07 construction
  arguments, G06 averaging, G07 stencil estimates, G06 common-pose assembly
  and Source/Drops insight; the G02 endpoint argument was inspected with
  local splitting. No remaining substantive local mathematical or main-step
  correspondence defect was found after making the signed covariance and
  reversed ordered derivative convention explicit.

  The shrinking-loop polynomial identity separates homogeneous radial jets:
  after multiplication by the highest mesh power and rounded-center passage,
  the highest curl vanishes; radial contraction recovers the homogeneous
  potential, and induction eliminates the remaining degrees. Finite dimension
  reduces the separating family to a finite panel. Orthogonal projection onto
  its invariant range gives an equivariant linear left inverse. The
  inverse-function theorem then gives an equivariant germ of recovery; no
  common invariant neighborhood is asserted. A shared Taylor polynomial
  preserves all prescribed jets, the unit, reality and constant gauge
  invariance. Signed averaging preserves these identities and enlarges the
  finite stencil only finitely.

  The corrected covariance is T(v)(pull_s U)=T(rho(s)v)(U). Reversal follows
  from the explicitly reversed transport ODE. An ordered list of left
  directions produces the reversed matrix product col(A_k)...col(A_1)U. Each
  differentiated or undifferentiated healthy slot supplies one threshold
  factor. Thus a column containing at least r slots has derivative price C
  t^r through any fixed finite order; multiplying by t^{-r} yields a bounded
  section price. The finite collection gives a common threshold and finite
  total price uniformly in the lattice period, with the stated stencil-fit
