  <section|Projected Gaussian differentiation: local
  pass><label|projected-gaussian-differentiation-local-pass>

  Parent gaussian-differentiation.tex SHA256
  c2dd6676b23cfd23302126415b828be0bddd2513da077173a56bb99282e7ed11 passes
  local conditional mathematical and source review. Read E6 Levan, Pendant,
  Mesoglea, Afresh, Tropolis, Meristem, Orin, Thistle, Paisley, Ether,
  Pinene, Fairum, Ambit and Mercury in full. Projection uses the actual
  principal covariance, not a principal precision or an independence
  assumption. The full-colour bond lens is preserved by the bondwise
  compensating diffeomorphism, so its positive finite-lens Jacobian gives the
  exact relative-density identity, including empty lenses.

  The determinant expansion uses principal minors and the vanishing order at
  the identity: derivatives through j only see minors of size at most j. The
  stated rank polynomial, j! and (1+v)^j prices match Pendant.jewels; the
  relative log determinant and quadratic form then give the density-jet
  consumer. This pointwise estimate is correctly separated from domination.
  The neighborhood envelope follows from a uniform inverse lower bound, a
  bounded displacement, and the inequality \|z\|^2\<less\>=2\|w\|^2+2B^2. Its
  reciprocal reference density cancels under integration, leaving a
  polynomial times a decaying Gaussian. The constants may depend on the fixed
  period; they are explicitly not offered as uniform RG prices.

  For actual activities, bounded displacement and Jacobian derivatives follow
  from the compactly supported compensation correction and the uniformly
  bounded unmasked mean family. Radial truncation is applied to initial
  physical fluctuation coordinates and leaves the held fine field unchanged.
  Nonzero protected masks force equality on the full action read support;
  linear scores vanish off hot bonds, and quadratic itineraries have hot
  endpoints. The latter is exact because A and C have identity inactive
  blocks, hence AC-1 has zero inactive rows and columns. Nearby principal
  invertibility, rather than nearby sector inequalities, suffices. The finite
  product argument retains the explicitly supplied bounds for frozen
  regular/forced activities and contractions.

  Finite-dimensional difference quotients give derivative measurability;
  dominated convergence and segment integration prove continuous derivative
  interchange on one common open neighborhood. The final sharp moment bound
  is a separate input, matching Mercury.coterie/FlareW rather than being
  supplied by the rough envelope. No gap found in this conditional
  differentiation argument. Commands cat/rg/wc/shasum and append-only review
  write; no build, metadata approval or source mutation.

